    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "An experimental and numerical investigation of heat transfer during the injection of hot water and steam into porous media was made. The unsteady state temperature distributions within the porous packs were experimentally determined during either hot water or steam injection by measuring the axial and sidewall temperatures of the pack during injection. Heating efficiency was calculated from these data. The hot fluid, steam or hot water, was injected into the porous pack at a constant rate and at constant temperature. A mathematical model of the convective-conductive heat flow process was formulated, and the model programmed into FORTRAN to simulate the process. The model used finite-difference techniques to set up the numerical scheme and the system of equations was solved by Line Successive Over-Relaxation. This model was used to simulate a variety of experimental data under similar injection conditions. From the experimental data, it was evident that the heating efficiency was dependent on the heat injection rate. Heating efficiency is defined as the fraction of the cumulative heat injected remaining in the injection interval. The data indicated that the higher the heat injection rates (Btu/hr) the more efficient was the process. For the hot-fluid mass rates investigated the data also indicated that the efficiency level was a function of these mass rates. This observation was contrary to all published theoretical studies which indicated no rate sensitivity of heating efficiency to injection mass rates. Using the mathematical model of the process, it was deter mined that the experimentally observed rate dependence of heating efficiency could not be explained by variations in thermal conductivity along the direction of flow. Further analysis showed that this rate dependence could be attributed to a mass rate sensitive film coefficient at the boundary of the sand and the adjacent formation. The variation of the heat transfer properties of this film coefficient account for the observed changes in heating efficiency. Finally, at the low mass fluxes used in field operations, it appears that this film coefficient will not be a significant factor affecting heating efficiency. The laboratory rates used were far in excess of any field rates.",
